                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Queen of the Seas is horrible.  Calling it a platformer falls somewhere between an outright lie and creative marketting.  There is no platforming because there are no platforms.\n\nAll you get is the ground, which goes slightly up, runs flat for a bit and then goes slightly down.  And really, that's it, there is nothing to navigate.  You walk right avoid the shark and the jelly fish instances and then complete the game.  The whole thing took me 22 minutes.\n\nThere is a bit of grinding at the start to get the first 50 coins for upgrades and then once you can jump into the floating clouds of coins the other upgrades come almost immediately.  But why were there upgrades at all?  The initial slow walk and tiny jump seem pointless and aren't fun.  Although nothing in this game is fun, the inital grind is less fun than the rest.\n\nThere is no hit bar once you've bought the kevlar upgrade so if you want to know how many hits you can take.  Well, tough luck.  (And how does kevlar even work under the ocean, how does it stop shark attacks?).  Why is there no death animation?  How - in a game that only has two enemies - is the collision detection so bad?\n\nI have so many questions and see so many poor design choices.\n\nWhy doesn't the player character duck whilst the walk animation is cycling?  Why don't any of the texture resolutions match?  Why doesn't the ground texture even tile properly?  That's seriously amateur.  Why is there no parallax effect on the background layer.  Why does the seaweed in the foreground scroll more slowly than the foreground?  Was that supposed to be the parallax effect?  If so then it's on the wrong layer.\n\nWhy do some of the sharks move stupidly fast?  Why do jellyfish pop-up out of the sand directly underneath the player with no warning?  Was this game even play tested at all?\n\nWhy don't keys work in the game menu?  Why doesn't the steam interface work?  Why do the coin based achievements not work?  I ask again: was this game even play tested?  Was this entire game written in an afternoon?  Why doesn't the difficulty ramp up as the distance decreases (what is the distance measured in? feet? meters? furlongs?).\n\nWhy are some of the \"achievments\" not really achievements?  Getting the hit by a shark doesn't seem like an achievement.  Getting hit by a shark 50 times is a failure not worthy of an award.\n\nBut probably my two biggest questions are: why does the player's head pop out of his diving helmet when jumping and why does the mermaid - that he is so rapturously persuing - look like Arnold Schwarzenegger?\n\nAvoid Queen of Seas at all cost, it's barely a mobile runner and looks and sounds awful.",
